---
// Owner's guide shown only on the Beverly Hills submarket page: how Beverly Hills' own rules (rent control,
// transfer taxes, earthquake retrofits, SB 79) differ from the City of Los Angeles. Sources, checked October 22, 2026:
// - Rent control: Beverly Hills has its own Rent Stabilization Ordinance. The Apartment Association of Greater Los
//   Angeles' notice of June 16, 2026 reports the city's updated allowable increases: Chapter 5 (tenant's initial rent
//   $600 or less, buildings built before September 20, 1978) 3.33% effective June 12, 2026 and updated monthly;
//   Chapter 6 (all other covered units) 3.6% effective in June, updated annually. Annual registration and the
//   statewide AB 1482 fallback for units not covered locally come from legal and city summaries that were not
//   re-read at the source, so the page tells owners to confirm with the city.
// - Transfer taxes: LA County Registrar-Recorder's Documentary Transfer Tax page (read in the browser). Only Culver
//   City, Los Angeles, Pomona, Redondo Beach, and Santa Monica have their own city rate; Beverly Hills is not listed,
//   so only the county's $1.10 per $1,000 applies. City of LA figures match the West Hollywood guide
//   (ULA 4% above $5.4M, 5.5% at $10.9M and up, $4.50 per $1,000 city tax).
// - Soft-story: City of Beverly Hills Seismic Retrofit Program, Ordinance 18-O-2767 (adopted December 11, 2018,
//   effective January 11, 2019), tiers by height and unit count. Taken from the city's program page as summarized in
//   search results (the page itself would not load), so no deadlines are given.
// - SB 79: Beverly Hills adopted an alternative plan by urgency Ordinance 26-O-2935; California's housing department
//   (HCD) sent the city a letter on September 3, 2026 about its revised plan, whose findings were not read.
// - D Line: LA Metro. The Wilshire/La Cienega station opened May 8, 2026; Metro's Beverly Hills and Century City
//   stations are projected for spring 2027 (an estimate, not a set date).
// Sales figures come from SALES_STATS, so they refresh with that file.
import PricePerUnitChart from './PricePerUnitChart.astro';
import { SALES_STATS, VALUATION_DATA_PERIOD } from '../data/valuationAssumptions';

const money = (n: number) => `$${Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-US')}`;

// ---- Transfer tax on the same building in the City of LA and in Beverly Hills (county tax only).
const laTax = (price: number) => {
	const county = (price / 1000) * 1.1;
	const city = (price / 1000) * 4.5;
	const ula = price > 5_400_000 ? price * (price >= 10_900_000 ? 0.055 : 0.04) : 0;
	return { county, city, ula, total: county + city + ula };
};
const scenarios = [6_000_000, 9_000_000].map((price) => {
	const la = laTax(price);
	const county = (price / 1000) * 1.1;
	return { price, la, bh: { county, total: county }, diff: la.total - county };
});

// ---- Allowed yearly rent increase, 2026 figures.
const CH6 = 3.6;
const CH5 = 3.33;

// ---- "Take your increase before you list": what an unclaimed Chapter 6 increase is worth at Beverly Hills cap rates.
const bhStats = SALES_STATS['beverly-hills'].rso;
const midCap = bhStats.capRate ? (bhStats.capRate.low + bhStats.capRate.high) / 2 : 0.05;
const EX_UNITS = 10;
const EX_RENT = 2000;
const extraYearly = EX_UNITS * EX_RENT * (CH6 / 100) * 12;
const extraValue = Math.round(extraYearly / midCap / 1000) * 1000;
---

<section class="section bhg">
	<div class="container bhg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Its Own City, Its Own Rules</span>
		<h2>Selling an Apartment Building in Beverly Hills vs. the City of Los Angeles</h2>
		<p>
			Beverly Hills is its own city, and that matters when you sell. It has its own rent stabilization law, its own yearly rent increase, no city
			transfer tax and no Measure ULA, its own earthquake retrofit program, and its own answer to the new state transit-housing law. A building on one
			side of the city line can face very different costs than the same building on the other.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="container">
		<figure class="bhg-compare" aria-labelledby="bhg-compare-cap">
			<figcaption id="bhg-compare-cap" class="bhg-figcap">Same kind of building, two sets of rules</figcaption>
			<div class="bhg-compare__grid" role="table" aria-describedby="bhg-compare-cap">
				<div class="bhg-compare__row bhg-compare__row--head" role="row">
					<span role="columnheader"></span>
					<span role="columnheader">City of Los Angeles</span>
					<span role="columnheader" class="bhg-compare__bh">Beverly Hills</span>
				</div>
				<div class="bhg-compare__row" role="row">
					<span role="rowheader">Rent control</span>
					<span role="cell">LA&rsquo;s ordinance, for buildings first occupied on or before Oct 1, 1978</span>
					<span role="cell" class="bhg-compare__bh">Beverly Hills&rsquo; own ordinance, in two chapters</span>
				</div>
				<div class="bhg-compare__row" role="row">
					<span role="rowheader">Yearly rent increase</span>
					<span role="cell">3% from July 1, 2026, set by the LA Housing Department</span>
					<span role="cell" class="bhg-compare__bh">{CH6}% for most units (Chapter 6) and {CH5}% for older low-rent units (Chapter 5), 2026 figures</span>
				</div>
				<div class="bhg-compare__row" role="row">
					<span role="rowheader">Transfer tax</span>
					<span role="cell">Measure ULA: 4% of the full price above $5.4M, 5.5% at $10.9M and up, plus a city tax of $4.50 per $1,000</span>
					<span role="cell" class="bhg-compare__bh"><strong>None</strong> beyond LA County&rsquo;s $1.10 per $1,000</span>
				</div>
				<div class="bhg-compare__row" role="row">
					<span role="rowheader">Earthquake retrofit</span>
					<span role="cell">LA&rsquo;s mandatory soft-story program</span>
					<span role="cell" class="bhg-compare__bh">Beverly Hills&rsquo; own program (Ordinance 18-O-2767, in force since January 2019)</span>
				</div>
				<div class="bhg-compare__row" role="row">
					<span role="rowheader">SB 79 transit housing law</span>
					<span role="cell">Phased in, with full implementation delayed to 2030 in historic districts and some other areas</span>
					<span role="cell" class="bhg-compare__bh">Adopted its own alternative plan by urgency ordinance (26-O-2935)</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</figure>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section section-alt bhg">
	<div class="container bhg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Beverly Hills Rent Control</span>
		<h2>Beverly Hills Rent Control, in Plain English</h2>
		<p>
			Beverly Hills runs its own Rent Stabilization Ordinance, separate from LA&rsquo;s. It is split into two chapters, and which one covers a unit depends
			on what the tenant paid when they moved in and when the building was built. Each chapter has its own yearly increase.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="container">
		<figure class="bhg-chapters" aria-labelledby="bhg-chapters-cap">
			<figcaption id="bhg-chapters-cap" class="bhg-figcap">Which chapter is my unit under?</figcaption>
			<div class="bhg-chapters__grid">
				<div class="bhg-chapters__box">
					<span class="bhg-chapters__tag">Chapter 5</span>
					<strong>Older, low-rent units</strong>
					<ul>
						<li>The tenant&rsquo;s initial rent at move-in was $600 or less</li>
						<li>The building was built before September 20, 1978</li>
						<li>Allowed increase: <strong>{CH5}%</strong> from June 12, 2026, updated every month</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="bhg-chapters__box bhg-chapters__box--main">
					<span class="bhg-chapters__tag">Chapter 6</span>
					<strong>All other covered units</strong>
					<ul>
						<li>The tenant&rsquo;s initial rent at move-in was more than $600</li>
						<li>Covers every other unit under the ordinance</li>
						<li>Allowed increase: <strong>{CH6}%</strong> from June 2026, updated once a year</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="bhg-source">Source: City of Beverly Hills allowable increases as reported by the Apartment Association of Greater Los Angeles, June 16, 2026.</p>
		</figure>
	</div>

	<div class="container bhg__narrow">
		<h3>Three rent control rules every Beverly Hills seller should know</h3>
		<ol class="bhg-rules">
			<li>
				<strong>Know which chapter each unit is under.</strong> Two units in the same building can have different yearly increases. Chapter 5&rsquo;s figure
				changes every month, so use the current number when you serve a notice, not last year&rsquo;s.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Registration is a yearly step.</strong> Beverly Hills asks owners of most rental properties to register their units with the city each
				year. Buyers and their attorneys will ask for proof, so have it ready.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Units outside the city&rsquo;s rules may still have a cap.</strong> Where Beverly Hills&rsquo; ordinance does not cover a unit, the statewide
				AB 1482 cap (5% plus inflation, up to 10%) may apply.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p class="bhg-source">
			Confirm how these rules apply to your building with the City of Beverly Hills at (310) 285-1000 or with a landlord-tenant attorney. The
			city updates its figures during the year.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section bhg">
	<div class="container bhg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Transfer Taxes</span>
		<h2>Beverly Hills Is Not Subject to Measure ULA</h2>
		<p>
			Measure ULA, often called the &ldquo;mansion tax,&rdquo; applies only to property in the City of Los Angeles. Beverly Hills is a separate city, so
			<strong>ULA does not apply to Beverly Hills apartment buildings</strong> at any price. Only five cities in LA County charge their own transfer tax,
			and Beverly Hills is not one of them. A seller there pays only LA County&rsquo;s documentary transfer tax of $1.10 per $1,000. Here is what that
			means at two prices.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="container">
		{
			scenarios.map((sc, idx) => (
				<figure class="bhg-tax" aria-labelledby={`bhg-tax-cap-${idx}`}>
					<figcaption id={`bhg-tax-cap-${idx}`} class="bhg-figcap">
						Transfer taxes on a {money(sc.price)} apartment building
					</figcaption>
					<div class="bhg-tax__row">
						<span class="bhg-tax__name">City of Los Angeles</span>
						<span class="bhg-tax__track">
							{sc.la.ula > 0 && <span class="bhg-tax__seg bhg-tax__seg--ula" style={`width: ${(sc.la.ula / sc.la.total) * 100}%`} title="Measure ULA" />}
							<span class="bhg-tax__seg bhg-tax__seg--city" style={`width: ${(sc.la.city / sc.la.total) * 100}%`} title="LA city transfer tax" />
							<span class="bhg-tax__seg bhg-tax__seg--county" style={`width: ${(sc.la.county / sc.la.total) * 100}%`} title="LA County transfer tax" />
						</span>
						<span class="bhg-tax__amt">{money(sc.la.total)}</span>
					</div>
					<div class="bhg-tax__row">
						<span class="bhg-tax__name">Beverly Hills</span>
						<span class="bhg-tax__track">
							<span class="bhg-tax__seg bhg-tax__seg--county" style={`width: ${Math.max((sc.bh.total / sc.la.total) * 100, 1.5)}%`} title="LA County transfer tax" />
						</span>
						<span class="bhg-tax__amt bhg-tax__amt--good">{money(sc.bh.total)}</span>
					</div>
					<p class="bhg-tax__callout">
						Difference: <strong>{money(sc.diff)}</strong> less in transfer taxes on the Beverly Hills side.
					</p>
				</figure>
			))
		}
		<ul class="bhg-tax__legend">
			<li><span class="bhg-tax__key bhg-tax__seg--ula" />Measure ULA (City of LA only)</li>
			<li><span class="bhg-tax__key bhg-tax__seg--city" />LA city transfer tax</li>
			<li><span class="bhg-tax__key bhg-tax__seg--county" />LA County transfer tax ($1.10 per $1,000)</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="bhg-source">
			Illustration only, before commissions and other closing costs. Rates from the LA County Registrar-Recorder and the City of Los Angeles Office of
			Finance (ULA thresholds for closings after June 30, 2026). Talk to your tax advisor about your own sale.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="container bhg__narrow">
		<p>
			You keep more of your price, and you do not have to price around the $5.4M line the way City of LA sellers do. A Beverly Hills building should be
			priced at what it is worth.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section section-alt bhg">
	<div class="container bhg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Fresh Advice for Owners</span>
		<h2>Three Moves to Make Before You List a Beverly Hills Building</h2>
	</div>

	<div class="container bhg-moves">
		<div class="bhg-move">
			<span class="bhg-move__num">1</span>
			<h3>Take this year&rsquo;s rent increase first</h3>
			<p>
				Buyers pay for the rent roll they see, not the one you could have had. If you have not taken this year&rsquo;s increase on each unit, check which
				chapter it is under and serve it before you list.
			</p>
			<div class="bhg-math" aria-label={`Example: ${EX_UNITS} Chapter 6 units at ${money(EX_RENT)} a month`}>
				<div class="bhg-math__line"><span>{EX_UNITS} units &times; {money(EX_RENT)} rent &times; {CH6}%</span><span>{money(extraYearly / 12)} / month</span></div>
				<div class="bhg-math__line"><span>Over a year</span><span>{money(extraYearly)}</span></div>
				<div class="bhg-math__line bhg-math__line--total">
					<span>Added value at a {(midCap * 100).toFixed(1)}% cap rate</span><span>about {money(extraValue)}</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="bhg-move">
			<span class="bhg-move__num">2</span>
			<h3>Put your soft-story file in one folder</h3>
			<p>
				Beverly Hills has its own mandatory soft-story program, adopted in December 2018 and in force since January 2019. It covers wood-frame buildings
				with weak or open ground floors (think tuck-under parking) permitted before 1978, and the city sorts buildings into tiers by height and unit
				count. Gather the city&rsquo;s notice, the engineer&rsquo;s plans, permits, and final sign-off so a buyer does not have to ask. Confirm your
				building&rsquo;s status with the city.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="bhg-move">
			<span class="bhg-move__num">3</span>
			<h3>Check the subway and SB 79 for your lot</h3>
			<p>
				The D Line&rsquo;s Wilshire/La Cienega station opened May 8, 2026, and Metro projects a second Beverly Hills station and a Century City station for
				spring 2027. SB 79, the state transit-housing law, took effect July 1, 2026. Beverly Hills adopted its own alternative plan by urgency ordinance,
				and California&rsquo;s housing department has been reviewing it. What applies to your parcel is a question for the city, and we are glad to price
				your building both ways.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section bhg">
	<div class="container bhg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Prices Across LA</span>
		<h2>What Beverly Hills Apartment Buildings Sell For</h2>
		<p>
			Older buildings in Beverly Hills (built before 1978) sold for a median of {money(bhStats.pricePerUnit!.median)} per unit in {VALUATION_DATA_PERIOD}, across
			{bhStats.pricePerUnit!.compCount} sales{
				bhStats.capRate ? `, with cap rates typically between ${(bhStats.capRate.low * 100).toFixed(2)}% and ${(bhStats.capRate.high * 100).toFixed(2)}%` : ''
			}. Here is how that compares with the other neighborhoods we cover.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="container">
		<PricePerUnitChart highlight="beverly-hills" />
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section section-alt bhg">
	<div class="container bhg__narrow">
		<span class="eyebrow">Seller Checklist</span>
		<h2>What to Gather Before Selling a Beverly Hills Apartment Building</h2>
		<ul class="bhg-check">
			<li>Current rent roll with each tenant&rsquo;s move-in date, move-in rent, and current rent</li>
			<li>Which rent control chapter each unit is under, and proof the building&rsquo;s yearly city registration is current</li>
			<li>Copies of the last rent increase notices served</li>
			<li>Soft-story paperwork: the city&rsquo;s notice, engineer&rsquo;s plans, permits, and sign-off (if your building is on the city&rsquo;s list)</li>
			<li>Your SB 721 balcony and stair inspection report, if the building has elevated wood balconies, decks, or stairs (see our <a href="/insights/sb-721-balcony-inspection-los-angeles-apartment-owners/">SB 721 guide</a>)</li>
			<li>Your parcel&rsquo;s SB 79 status from the city</li>
			<li>Two years of operating expenses and utility bills</li>
			<li>Any buyout agreements, relocation payments, or pending tenant matters</li>
			<li>Leases, including any pet, parking, or storage agreements</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Not sure where to start? We will walk you through it as part of a free Broker Opinion of Value. <a href="/contact/?intent=bov">Request yours here</a>.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>

